VERDICT
Correct: your code iterates through the string in-place and correctly converts every uppercase ASCII letter to lowercase by adding 32 in linear time and constant auxiliary memory.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well:

You modified the string in-place instead of creating a second string buffer.

You properly targeted only uppercase letters using character range comparisons (s[i] >= 'A' && s[i] <= 'Z').

You correctly used the ASCII numerical difference between uppercase and lowercase letters (32).

Is it correct?

Yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses:

There are no logical bugs under LeetCode constraints (1 <= s.length <= 100, consisting of printable ASCII characters).

Style improvement: using s[i] += 32 or s[i] += ('a' - 'A') makes the intent clearer than hardcoding the raw magic number 32.

Compiler warning: comparing signed int i with s.length() (which returns an unsigned size_t) can produce signed/unsigned comparison warnings.

Edge cases handled properly: strings with no uppercase letters (e.g., "hello"), strings with only uppercase letters (e.g., "HELLO"), strings with digits, symbols, and spaces (e.g., "LO@# 12"), and single-character strings.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason:

Time complexity: O(n) where n is the length of s, because the loop visits each character in the string exactly once.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because characters are updated in-place without allocating extra heap memory.

Why my approach works:

Take s = "Hello". In ASCII, 'H' has code 72 and 'h' has code 104. 72 + 32 equals 104, transforming 'H' into 'h'. Lowercase letters like 'e', 'l', 'o' fail the range check and remain untouched, leaving "hello".

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Out-of-Place String Building (Brute Force)

Where it comes from:

Standard beginner baseline found in introductory programming textbooks and LeetCode discussion forums.

Idea:

Create a brand new empty string. Scan each character of the input string. If it is an uppercase letter, convert it and push it to the new string; otherwise, push the character as is.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string toLowerCase(string s) {
        string result = "";
        for (char c : s) {
            if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') {
                result += (c + 32);
            } else {
                result += c;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because each character is examined once.

Space Complexity:

O(n) auxiliary space to allocate and store the separate output string.

What makes the next approach better:

In C++, strings are mutable. We can mutate the input string directly in-place to avoid allocating an extra string buffer.

Approach 2: In-Place ASCII Offset Arithmetic (Your Approach)

Where it comes from:

LeetCode editorial and classic C-style low-level string manipulation.

Idea:

Loop through the string. In the ASCII table, 'a' is 97 and 'A' is 65. The difference 'a' - 'A' is exactly 32. If a character lies between 'A' and 'Z', add 32 to turn it lowercase in-place.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string toLowerCase(string s) {
        for (char &c : s) {
            if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') {
                c += ('a' - 'A');
            }
        }
        return s;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) single pass over the string.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space mutating in-place.

What makes the next approach better:

Instead of manually checking ranges and adding 32, we can use bitwise operations, which mirror how binary ASCII codes are organized.

Approach 3: Bitwise Manipulation (Low-Level Optimization)

Where it comes from:

Top-voted LeetCode discussion posts and competitive programming bitwise tricks.

Idea:

In binary, 'A' is 01000001 (65) and 'a' is 01100001 (97). The only difference between uppercase and lowercase ASCII letters is bit 5 (value 32, or binary 00100000). Setting bit 5 turns uppercase into lowercase. Since bit 5 for space (' ') is 32, doing c |= 32 (or c |= ' ') turns 'A'-'Z' into 'a'-'z'.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string toLowerCase(string s) {
        for (char &c : s) {
            if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') {
                c |= 32;
            }
        }
        return s;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) single pass over all characters.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space.

What makes the next approach better:

The standard library provides idiomatic C++ functions (std::tolower and std::transform) that are standard practice, readable, and locale-aware.

Approach 4: Standard Library std::transform with std::tolower (Idiomatic Modern C++)

Where it comes from:

Modern C++ standard library best practices using the algorithm and cctype headers.

Idea:

Use std::transform to apply std::tolower to each character in-place. Cast each character to unsigned char to avoid undefined behavior on negative char values.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cctype>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string toLowerCase(string s) {
        transform(s.begin(), s.end(), s.begin(), [](unsigned char c) {
            return tolower(c);
        });
        return s;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) single pass through the iterator range.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space since it writes directly back to s.begin().

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use?

You used Approach 2 (In-Place ASCII Offset Arithmetic).

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?

Interviewers usually expect Approach 2 or Approach 3. They ask this question to test if you know how ASCII values work and that you can implement tolower manually without built-in functions. Knowing that 'a' - 'A' == 32 and understanding the bitwise trick (Approach 3) demonstrates solid foundational knowledge.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:

Question 1: Why is the difference between uppercase and lowercase letters exactly 32?

Answer: ASCII designers deliberately spaced uppercase and lowercase letters by 32 (2 to the power of 5). That way, bit 5 is 0 for uppercase letters and 1 for lowercase letters, allowing conversion with a single bitwise OR operation (c |= 32).

Question 2: Why do we cast to unsigned char before calling std::tolower(c)?

Answer: std::tolower expects an int that can be represented as an unsigned char or EOF. On platforms where char is signed, negative values (like extended ASCII characters) cause undefined behavior unless cast to unsigned char first.

Question 3: What if the string contains non-English or Unicode characters (e.g., accented characters or German 'ß')?

Answer: ASCII arithmetic only covers basic English A-Z. For full Unicode text, standard ASCII tricks fail (for example, German 'ß' becomes "SS" in uppercase, changing string length). Unicode case mapping requires a dedicated Unicode library like ICU (International Components for Unicode) or std::wstring_convert with proper locale handling.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence:

Lowercase and uppercase ASCII letters differ by exactly 32, which corresponds to setting bit 5 (00100000).

My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:

Use ('a' - 'A') instead of hardcoding 32 to make the mathematical intent self-explanatory.

Prefer range-based reference loops (for (char &c : s)) over index-based loops when indices are not strictly needed.

Remember that bitwise OR (c |= 32) turns uppercase ASCII into lowercase.

The pattern or idea this question is really about:

ASCII encoding table properties and character manipulation.

